/**
@page extra_docs Extra Documentation and Design Notes

@section ext_t1 Graph Representation and Parsing

@subsection ext_t1_data Data model

The implementation separates the problem into four layers:

- @ref ProgramPoint "ProgramPoint": one program line plus an optional `+` / `-` marker.
- @ref LiveRange "LiveRange": one input line from the ranges file.
- @ref Web "Web": a merged set of live ranges for the same variable.
- `Graph<int>`: the interference graph, where each vertex stores a web id.

The provided graph structure from the course was preserved as the primary graph
representation. The only conceptual adaptation is that vertex labels are `int`
web identifiers instead of domain objects. This keeps the graph reusable and
lets the allocation algorithms work with a compact vertex set.

@subsection ext_t1_webs Web construction

The input file may contain multiple live ranges for the same variable. Those
ranges are merged into a web whenever they share at least one program point.
The implementation uses a union-find structure over the live ranges of a single
variable:

- Build one union-find element per live range.
- For every pair of ranges of the same variable, test whether they overlap.
- Union overlapping pairs.
- Emit one web per connected component.

Two implementation details matter for correctness and testing:

- The emitted webs are sorted deterministically by variable name and first live
  line.
- The final web ids are reassigned after sorting so output files are stable.

@subsection ext_t1_interference Interference rule

Two webs interfere if they are simultaneously live at some program point.
The implementation handles the register-reuse exception for adjacent live
ranges:

- if one web ends on line `L` due to a last use (`-`) and another web starts on
  the same line `L` due to a definition (`+`), they do **not** interfere.

This rule is implemented explicitly in
@ref InterferenceGraph::interferes "InterferenceGraph::interferes".

@subsection ext_t1_output Aggregated web output

The output format uses **one line per web**, not one line per constituent live
range. Internally, this means:

- collect all program points from all ranges of the web;
- sort them by line number;
- collapse duplicate line numbers;
- if the same line contains both `+` and `-`, treat it as an internal merge
  point and print it without a marker.

This preserves the intended fused-web representation: each final web has one
stable id and one sorted program-point list.

For the alternative algorithms, the writer appends a small processing-friendly
metadata section after the assignment section. The base format remains
unchanged, and tools can parse the extra records line-by-line:

- spilling/free records: `spills: N`, followed by `spill: webX`;
- splitting records: `splits: N`, followed by `split: webX -> webY,webZ`.

The split record names the source web selected by the heuristic and the two
derived web ids present in the final web list. The implementation reuses the
source id for the left derived web and allocates a new id to the right derived
web, making the mapping deterministic and easy to inspect.

In addition to the text output, the application can export a colored DOT graph
for the final allocation. Register-assigned webs are filled with register
colors, memory-assigned webs are gray boxes, and split-derived webs have a
highlighted border. Batch mode writes this graph automatically next to the
allocation file using the same base name and a `.dot` extension.

@subsection ext_t1_complexity Complexity

| Step | Complexity |
|------|-----------|
| Parse ranges | `O(L * P)` |
| Parse config | `O(L)` |
| Build webs | `O(V * R^2 * P log P)` |
| Build interference graph | `O(W^2 * P + E * W)` |

The bounds above describe the current implementation. The extra `log P` in web
construction comes from set-based overlap tests, and the `E * W` graph-building
term comes from the vector-backed course graph, where endpoint lookup is linear
in the number of webs.

---

@section ext_t2 T2.1 - Basic Register Allocation

@subsection ext_t2_basic_approach Approach

The basic allocator uses a simplify/select structure:

- Maintain the set of active vertices.
- Repeatedly remove every active vertex with degree `< K` and push it onto a
  stack.
- If no such vertex exists, pick a spill candidate heuristically and remove it.
- Pop the stack and greedily assign the first available register.

In the **basic** mode, any spill means the allocation is considered infeasible.
The service layer then converts the result into the specification-compliant
fallback output:

- `registers: 0`
- every web mapped to `M`

@subsection ext_t2_basic_complexity Complexity

| Step | Complexity |
|------|-----------|
| Simplification + select | `O(W * (W^2 + E))` |

The `W^2` component comes from repeated `findVertex` calls on the provided
vector-backed graph. With an indexed graph representation, this would reduce to
the usual simplify/select adjacency-scan cost.

---

@section ext_t3 T2.2 - Register Allocation with Spilling

@subsection ext_t3_spill_approach Approach

For the spilling mode, spilling is no longer treated as a failure. The algorithm:

- tries to color the graph with zero forced spills;
- if that fails, forces one spill and retries;
- continues until either a valid coloring is found or the configured bound `K`
  is exhausted.

The spill heuristic is deterministic and pressure-oriented:

- prefer the currently highest-degree web;
- break ties by lower web id to keep results deterministic.

When a valid coloring is found, only the selected spilled webs are mapped to
memory. Unlike the basic mode, the result remains feasible.

The output metadata records the exact web ids selected by this heuristic, for
example `spills: 1` followed by `spill: web0`.

@subsection ext_t3_spill_example Example

The triangle below needs 3 colors, but with `K = 2` registers it becomes
allocatable after spilling one web:

<div class="interactive_dotgraph">

\dotfile dox/example_spilling.dot "Triangle graph: one web must be spilled for K = 2"

</div>

@subsection ext_t3_spill_complexity Complexity

| Step | Complexity |
|------|-----------|
| Up to `K` recoloring attempts | `O((K + 1) * W * (W^2 + E))` |

---

@section ext_t4 T2.3 - Register Allocation with Splitting

@subsection ext_t4_split_approach Approach

Web splitting is used when the user explicitly selects `splitting, K`.
The implementation keeps the problem local and deterministic:

- first, try to color the current graph with no splitting;
- if that fails, pick one of the currently uncolorable/high-degree webs;
- split it into two derived webs;
- rebuild the interference graph;
- retry coloring;
- stop after at most `K` successful split operations.

Two split strategies are used:

- if the original web already contains multiple ranges, split those ranges into
  two ordered groups;
- if it contains only one range, split the sequence of program points into two
  contiguous derived ranges.

The split operation deliberately preserves the original markers. A boundary
created by the allocator is not a new definition and is not a new last use. For
example, splitting `1+,2,3,4-` may produce `1+,2` and `3,4-`, never `1+,2-` and
`3+,4-`.

Candidates whose two derived webs would still interfere with each other are
discarded, because such a split would not represent two non-overlapping live
pieces of the value.

Candidate selection is also more deliberate than "split the first failed web":
for every legal split position, the allocator rebuilds the graph and tests
K-colorability. It chooses a split that makes the graph colorable if one exists;
otherwise it chooses the split that leaves the fewest interference edges, then
the smallest maximum degree. This keeps the heuristic understandable while still
targeting the pressure that actually blocks coloring.

The output metadata records every performed split as
`split: webSource -> webLeft,webRight`, which identifies the selected source
web and the derived webs that define the save/restore boundaries.

@subsection ext_t4_split_example Example

The following case starts as a 3-clique. `web0` has an early region and a late
region; after splitting it, the resulting graph becomes a 2-colorable path:

<div class="interactive_dotgraph">

\dotfile dox/example_splitting.dot "One split reduces the chromatic requirement without inventing markers"

</div>

@subsection ext_t4_split_complexity Complexity

| Step | Complexity |
|------|-----------|
| Candidate evaluation in one split iteration | `O(Q * (W^2 * P + E * W + W * (W^2 + E)))` |
| Full allocator with at most `K` splits | `O(K * Q * (W^2 * P + E * W + W * (W^2 + E)))` |

Here `Q` is the number of legal split positions currently available.

---

@section ext_t5 T2.4 - Custom Allocation Strategy

@subsection ext_t5_free_approach Approach

The custom mode is a hybrid allocator:

- edgeless graphs are assigned one register, because no webs interfere;
- complete graphs color at most `K` webs and spill the unavoidable remainder;
- bipartite graphs are recognized and 2-colored by BFS when `K >= 2`;
- all other graphs use DSatur ordering: choose the uncolored web with the largest
  number of distinct neighbor colors, breaking ties by degree;
- for small/medium graphs, a branch-and-bound refinement searches for a coloring
  with fewer spills than the DSatur result.

This keeps the technique recognizable from standard graph coloring material:
BFS for bipartite graphs, direct handling for cliques, DSatur for practical
greedy coloring, and branch-and-bound for bounded exact improvement. The
invariant remains simple: no two interfering colored webs may share a register;
memory is used only for webs left uncolored by the selected strategy.

@subsection ext_t5_free_complexity Complexity

| Step | Complexity |
|------|-----------|
| Graph-class checks and adjacency extraction | `O(W^2 + E log W)` |
| DSatur fallback | `O(W^2 + E log W)` |
| Guarded branch-and-bound refinement | `O((K + 1)^W * (W + E))` worst case |

The exact refinement is guarded by a fixed graph-size threshold. Larger graphs
return the polynomial DSatur result so the application remains responsive during
the demo.

